@topic stdlib.module.abi
title: -
summary: Fixed-width ABI integer types (abi::I8 ... abi::Usize): Ints proven in a foreign interface's range, made by checked creators such as abi::u32(n).
index: stdlib
keywords: fixed width integers; int32; uint8; c integer; abi types
related: concept.integer-domains stdlib.module.abi::bytes stdlib.module.ffi::c

== NOTES
These are domains, not machine arithmetic: an abi::U8 is an ordinary Int
with the proof that it lies in 0..255, held in the struct's one field
`value`. Each type is a nominal struct, so abi::u8(255) + 1 is the TYPE
error, never 0 or 256: compute in Int (x.value), and re-enter a domain
through its creator. abi::I32 and abi::U32 are distinct even where they
overlap, as are abi::I64 and abi::Isize. Machine meaning (sign or zero
extension into a register) starts only at an architecture layer,
abi::x86_64::from_i32 and its siblings. C's own integer types are
ffi::c's.

@topic stdlib.module.abi::bytes
title: -
summary: Bytes and MutableBytes: owned byte sequences, immutable and writable, that foreign boundaries (syscalls, C calls) take, with their construction and snapshots.
index: stdlib
keywords: byte array; byte buffer; binary data; raw bytes; bytes type
related: stdlib.str::encode_utf8 stdlib.module.binary stdlib.module.linux

== NOTES
`import abi::bytes`. A Bytes is an immutable, finite sequence of bytes
with an exact length: not a String, not a C string (a 0 is an ordinary
byte and nothing terminates it), not a pointer or a view. A MutableBytes
has a fixed length and changeable contents, and is a value too: replace
returns a new value and every logical copy stays independent. Both are
opaque structs, compared and hashed by their bytes; only this module can
open or build them. Make Bytes from a List[byte::Byte] (for text,
str::encode_utf8(s) gives one). There is deliberately no indexing,
slicing, iteration or concatenation of Bytes.

@topic stdlib.abi::bytes::Bytes
title: -
summary: An immutable, owned sequence of bytes with an exact length (an opaque struct), made by abi::bytes::from_list and compared and hashed by its bytes.
index: stdlib
keywords: bytes; byte string; binary data; immutable buffer
related: stdlib.abi::bytes::from_list stdlib.abi::bytes::length stdlib.binary::parse stdlib.abi::bytes::MutableBytes

== NOTES
Its one field is private to module abi::bytes (a projection or a
construction elsewhere is OPAQUE-REPRESENTATION / OPAQUE-CONSTRUCTION).
Copy it, store it, compare it: no address, sharing or allocation is
observable. The machine address of its payload exists only through the
native-only bridge abi::x86_64::from_bytes (or its AArch64 twin).

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import abi::bytes
import str

a = abi::bytes::from_list("ab".encode_utf8())
[
    a == abi::bytes::from_list("ab".encode_utf8()),
    abi::bytes::length(a).value
]
@value [true, 2]
@end

@topic stdlib.abi::bytes::MutableBytes
title: -
summary: A fixed-length byte sequence whose contents can change, as a value: updates return a new MutableBytes and logical copies stay independent (an opaque struct).
index: stdlib
keywords: mutable bytes; writable buffer; byte buffer; read buffer
related: stdlib.abi::bytes::zeroed stdlib.abi::bytes::replace stdlib.abi::bytes::freeze stdlib.abi::bytes::Bytes

== NOTES
Made by abi::bytes::zeroed(n) or from_bytes(data); read back with freeze
or freeze_prefix. It is not a reference: `b = a` and then
`a2 = abi::bytes::replace(a, 0, x)` leave a and b unchanged. Compared and
hashed by its current bytes. A foreign write (linux::read) works on a
detached private copy and returns it.

@topic stdlib.binary::U8
title: -
summary: The unsigned 8-bit layout field type: Int in 0..255, one byte.
index: stdlib
keywords: uint8; unsigned byte field; layout field type
related: stdlib.module.binary concept.binary-layouts

== NOTES
An ordinary integer domain: a layout field's encoding follows from its
type's interval, so byte::Byte or abi::U8Value serve as well. Every
binary:: scalar is little-endian.

