@topic stdlib.module.byte
title: -
summary: The Byte, Nibble, LowNibble and HighNibble integer domains, the checked conversion byte::from_int, nibble extraction, complement and byte::set.
index: stdlib
keywords: byte; nibble; bits; byte type; uint8
related: concept.integer-domains stdlib.bit_and stdlib.module.ascii

== NOTES
Ordinary Botlish over the bit primitives (bit_and, bit_or, bit_xor,
shift_left, shift_right): each function has a declared result domain the
compiler verifies. An Int becomes a byte::Byte where the compiler proves
its range (a comparison, a literal, a declared result) or through
byte::from_int. Outside the module, `import type byte::Byte` binds the
short type name. ASCII meaning is ascii::'s, not byte::'s.

@topic stdlib.byte::Byte
title: -
summary: The integer domain 0..255 (type Byte = Int in 0..255): a byte value, an ordinary Int proven in range.
index: stdlib
keywords: byte; uint8; octet; byte type; 0..255
related: stdlib.byte::from_int concept.integer-domains stdlib.str::encode_utf8 stdlib.abi::bytes::from_list

== NOTES
A Byte is an Int, so every Int operation applies, and its arithmetic is
Int arithmetic (a sum of two Bytes is an Int, not a wrapped Byte). An Int
enters the domain where the compiler proves 0 <= x <= 255 -- a literal, a
comparison guard, a function's declared `-> Byte` result -- or through
byte::from_int, which fails otherwise. str::encode_utf8 returns
List[Byte]; abi::bytes::from_list takes one.

@topic stdlib.byte::Nibble
title: -
summary: The integer domain 0..15 (a subtype of Byte): a logical 4-bit value.
index: stdlib
keywords: nibble; half byte; 4-bit value; hex digit value
related: stdlib.byte::nibble stdlib.byte::LowNibble stdlib.byte::HighNibble

@topic stdlib.byte::LowNibble
title: -
summary: The integer domain 0..15 for a nibble already positioned in the low half of a byte; nominally distinct from Nibble.
index: stdlib
keywords: low nibble; low half; lower 4 bits
related: stdlib.byte::low_nibble stdlib.byte::position_low stdlib.byte::Nibble

@topic stdlib.byte::HighNibble
title: -
summary: The 16 values 0, 16, 32, ..., 240: a nibble positioned in the high half of a byte.
index: stdlib
keywords: high nibble; high half; upper 4 bits
related: stdlib.byte::high_nibble stdlib.byte::position_high stdlib.byte::Nibble

@topic stdlib.byte::BelowRange
title: -
summary: The declared error byte::from_int fails with for a negative Int; handled as `on byte::BelowRange:`.
index: stdlib
keywords: below range; negative; too small; byte conversion error
related: stdlib.byte::from_int stdlib.byte::AboveRange

@topic stdlib.byte::AboveRange
title: -
summary: The declared error byte::from_int fails with for an Int above 255 (and byte::set for a character above U+00FF); handled as `on byte::AboveRange:`.
index: stdlib
keywords: above range; too large; byte conversion error
related: stdlib.byte::from_int stdlib.byte::set stdlib.byte::BelowRange

@topic stdlib.byte::high_nibble
title: -
summary: The high half of a byte kept in position, bit_and(b, 240): a byte::HighNibble (171 gives 160).
index: stdlib
keywords: high nibble; upper bits; mask high half
related: stdlib.byte::nibble stdlib.byte::low_nibble stdlib.byte::HighNibble

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import byte

b = byte::from_int(171)
[
    byte::high_nibble(b),
    byte::low_nibble(b),
    byte::nibble(byte::high_nibble(b)),
    byte::complement(b)
]
@value [160, 11, 10, 84]
@caption 171 is 0xAB
@end

@topic stdlib.byte::low_nibble
title: -
summary: The low half of a byte, bit_and(b, 15): a byte::LowNibble.
index: stdlib
keywords: low nibble; lower bits; mask low half
related: stdlib.byte::high_nibble stdlib.byte::nibble stdlib.byte::LowNibble

@topic stdlib.byte::nibble
title: -
summary: The logical 0..15 value of a positioned nibble (high or low half of a byte): a byte::Nibble.
index: stdlib
keywords: nibble value; hex digit value; normalize nibble
related: stdlib.byte::high_nibble stdlib.byte::low_nibble stdlib.byte::Nibble

== NOTES
One branch-free formula for both halves: a low-positioned x (x <= 15) is
returned unchanged, a high-positioned one (low 4 bits zero) is shifted
down, so nibble(high_nibble(b)) is b's first hexadecimal digit.

@topic stdlib.byte::complement
title: -
summary: The 8-bit one's complement of a byte, 255 - b: a byte::Byte (not Int's infinite-precision complement).
index: stdlib
keywords: invert bits; bitwise not; one's complement; flip byte
related: stdlib.bit_xor stdlib.byte::Byte

@topic stdlib.byte::position_low
title: -
summary: A Nibble as a LowNibble: the same number, now marked as positioned in the low half of a byte.
index: stdlib
keywords: place low nibble; low half
related: stdlib.byte::position_high stdlib.byte::LowNibble

@topic stdlib.byte::position_high
title: -
summary: A Nibble moved into the high half of a byte, n times 16: a byte::HighNibble (11 gives 176).
index: stdlib
keywords: place high nibble; shift nibble; high half
related: stdlib.byte::position_low stdlib.byte::HighNibble

@topic stdlib.byte::from_int
title: -
summary: The Int as a byte::Byte, or the declared error byte::BelowRange (negative) or byte::AboveRange (above 255): the checked conversion into the Byte domain.
index: stdlib
keywords: int to byte; to byte; checked conversion; narrow to byte
related: stdlib.byte::Byte stdlib.byte::BelowRange stdlib.byte::AboveRange rule.known-error

== NOTES
Never truncates or wraps. The completion proof works per call: a
provably in-range argument (a literal 48, a value guarded to 0..255)
needs no handler, and a provably out-of-range constant (999) is
rejected as KNOWN-ERROR; any other Int keeps both errors to handle or
admit. Its parameter is declared `value: int`, so the argument must be
proven an Int.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import byte

fn clamp(n: int) -> int:
    b = byte::from_int(n):
        on byte::BelowRange:
            return 0
        on byte::AboveRange:
            return 255
    b

[byte::from_int(65), clamp(300), clamp(-3), clamp(7)]
@value [65, 255, 0, 7]
@end

@topic stdlib.byte::set
title: -
summary: The ImmutableSet[Byte] of the characters' Unicode scalar values (not their UTF-8 bytes); a character above U+00FF fails with byte::AboveRange.
index: stdlib
keywords: byte set; character set; set of bytes
related: stdlib.immutable_set::from_list stdlib.char::scalar_value stdlib.byte::from_int

== NOTES
Each character becomes the one byte equal to its scalar value, so 'é'
(U+00E9) is 233, never the two UTF-8 bytes. No partial set: the first
character outside Byte's domain ends the call. A literal List of
characters in range needs no handler.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import byte
import immutable_set

s = byte::set(['a', 'é'])
[s.contains(97), s.contains(233), s.contains(98)]
@value [true, true, false]
@end

@topic stdlib.module.ascii
title: -
summary: Structural ASCII classification of byte values: is_digit, is_upper, is_lower, is_alphabetic and is_alphanumeric over byte::Byte.
index: stdlib
keywords: ascii; character class; isdigit; isalpha; classify bytes
related: stdlib.byte::Byte stdlib.str::encode_utf8 stdlib.str::is_tcl_alpha

== NOTES
Every predicate takes a byte::Byte and is total: a byte of 128..255 is
simply not ASCII (false), never an error. Locale-, encoding- and
platform-independent: no libc classes, no Unicode categories, no case
folding. Classify a String by its UTF-8 bytes (str::encode_utf8) or a
character by its scalar value once that is proven below 128.

@topic stdlib.ascii::is_digit
title: -
summary: Whether a byte is an ASCII decimal digit, '0'..'9' (48..57).
index: stdlib
keywords: is digit; isdigit; numeric character
related: stdlib.module.ascii stdlib.ascii::is_alphanumeric

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import ascii
import list
import str

fn digits(s: str) -> int:
    hits = loop b in s.encode_utf8():
        if not ascii::is_digit(b):
            continue
        b
    hits.length()

digits("a1b22")
@value 3
@end

