@topic stdlib.web::is_unreserved
title: -
summary: Whether a byte is an RFC 3986 unreserved character: an ASCII letter or digit, or one of - . _ ~.
index: stdlib
keywords: unreserved; url safe character; uri character
related: stdlib.web::uri_escape_text stdlib.ascii::is_alphanumeric

@topic stdlib.web::UriQueryValue
title: -
summary: A String proven safe to embed as a URI query value by web::uri_query_value? (a refinement of str).
index: stdlib
keywords: url query value; escaped string; query parameter
related: stdlib.web::uri_query_value? stdlib.web::uri_escape_text concept.refinements

@topic stdlib.web::uri_escape_text
title: -
summary: The String percent-encoded for a URI: every UTF-8 byte except A-Z a-z 0-9 - . _ ~ written as uppercase %XX.
index: stdlib
keywords: url encode; percent encode; uri escape; urlencode; escape query
related: stdlib.web::uri_query_value? stdlib.web::is_unreserved

== NOTES
A space becomes %20 and "é" %C3%A9 (its two UTF-8 bytes). The result is a
plain str: like every String transformation it carries no refinement, so
to pass it where a web::UriQueryValue is required, prove it with
web::uri_query_value?, which accepts every String this function writes.

@topic stdlib.module.linux::sys
title: -
summary: The architecture's Linux syscall numbers and register convention under lib/linux.bot's wrappers: linux::sys::write and read (a direct module).
index: stdlib
keywords: syscall numbers; linux system calls; raw write; raw read
related: stdlib.module.linux concept.direct-modules backend.native-only

== NOTES
An architecture-specific module: the compilation target selects
lib/linux/sys.x86_64.bot or lib/linux/sys.aarch64.bot, which declare the
same members. It is a direct module (raw authority, no context), and its
syscalls run only on the native backends. Programs use linux::write and
linux::read, or the portable io.

@topic stdlib.module.io
title: -
summary: Portable text output -- io::print, print_line, write_error and write_error_line -- through the io::IO context trait; with linux::io it runs on the native backends.
index: stdlib
keywords: print; output; stdout; stderr; write text; console
related: task.print-output backend.io concept.context-traits

== NOTES
The operations require the io::IO context trait, which a program
satisfies by installing a context that implements it, normally
`with context linux::io::create()`. That standard implementation writes
with raw Linux syscalls, so printing runs only on the native backends
(cranelift, cranelift-generic, executables); on interp and compile the
first write fails with NATIVE-ONLY. On every backend the program's final
value is still printed by main.tcl. There is no input, file or
formatting API here.

@topic stdlib.linux::path::LinuxPath
title: -
summary: A String proven to be a valid textual Linux path -- non-empty, no U+0000 -- by linux::path::valid? or validate; it claims nothing about the filesystem.
index: stdlib
keywords: path; file path; filename; linux path type
related: stdlib.linux::path::valid? stdlib.linux::path::validate concept.refinements capability.files

== NOTES
A refinement of str with a String's representation, so a validated value
is still used as text. It does not mean the path exists, is accessible,
canonical or short enough: validation looks at no filesystem and does not
normalize ("a//b" stays as written). Botlish has no API that opens a path
(capability.files).

