index: stdlib
keywords: empty vector; typed empty vector
related: stdlib.mutable_vector::from_list stdlib.module.test

@topic stdlib.test::no_results
title: -
summary: An empty MutableVector[test::TestResult]: the test framework's typed empty vector of results.
index: stdlib
keywords: empty vector; test results
related: stdlib.mutable_vector::from_list stdlib.test::results

@topic stdlib.linux::write
title: -
summary: One write(2) of a Bytes value to file descriptor fd, returning the kernel's raw signed result (bytes written, or -errno); native backends only.
index: stdlib
keywords: write bytes; write syscall; file descriptor; raw output
related: stdlib.module.linux stdlib.module.io concept.direct-modules backend.native-only

== NOTES
fd is an abi::I32 (abi::i32(1) is standard output) and data an
abi::bytes::Bytes (for text, abi::bytes::from_list(s.encode_utf8())).
Exactly one syscall: no retry and no loop, so a positive result smaller
than the length is a short write the caller must continue, and a
negative one is the kernel's -errno, unchanged (-9 for a bad
descriptor). module linux is direct: the caller must be a `direct
module` or mediate the call through a context of its own. Portable code
prints with io::print_line instead.

@topic stdlib.linux::sys::write
title: -
summary: write(2) through the target architecture's transport: the syscall number and registers for writing data to fd, the raw result as an Int; native backends only.
index: stdlib
keywords: write syscall; sys_write
related: stdlib.linux::write stdlib.module.linux::sys stdlib.linux::abi::x86_64::syscall

== NOTES
The architecture layer under linux::write (on x86-64: SYS_write = 1, rdi
fd, rsi the payload's address, rdx its length). Call linux::write
instead; the two have the same contract.

@topic stdlib.linux::sys::read
title: -
summary: read(2) through the target architecture's transport into a freshly detached copy of the buffer: the raw result and the copy; native backends only.
index: stdlib
keywords: read syscall; sys_read
related: stdlib.linux::read stdlib.module.linux::sys stdlib.abi::bytes::detach

== NOTES
The architecture layer under linux::read (on x86-64: SYS_read = 0). The
kernel writes into a private copy of data, never into storage another
value can observe. Call linux::read instead, which returns a
linux::ReadResult.

@topic stdlib.test::Suite
title: -
summary: The test suite context: the registered cases, in order, installed with `with context test::suite()` and filled by test::value, test::rejects and their siblings.
index: stdlib
keywords: test suite; test context; test registry
related: concept.testing stdlib.test::suite stdlib.test::report

== NOTES
A context struct whose one field is a MutableVector of the registered
cases. Only test::case and test::registered take it as their context
parameter; every function that registers or runs tests requires it by
derivation, so a test program installs one suite before registering.

@topic stdlib.test::run
title: -
summary: The runner coroutine: it runs one registered case per resume, in registration order, yielding each result as a one-element List and the empty List at the end.
index: stdlib
keywords: test runner; run tests
related: stdlib.test::results stdlib.test::report concept.coroutines concept.testing

== NOTES
Start it with `coroutine {step, first} = test::run()` and resume it with
step(). Most programs call test::report() (or test::results()), which
drive it to the end.

@topic stdlib.module.linux
title: -
summary: Thin Botlish wrappers over the raw Linux syscall transports: linux::write and linux::read, one syscall each with the kernel's raw result (a direct module, native only).
index: stdlib
keywords: linux syscalls; raw io; file descriptor; write; read
related: stdlib.module.linux::io stdlib.module.linux::sys concept.direct-modules backend.native-only

== NOTES
The same source on every architecture: linux::sys, chosen by the
compilation target, holds the syscall numbers. A direct module: code that
calls it must be a `direct module` or mediate the call through a context
of its own (linux::io does, with LinuxIO). Every call runs only on the
native backends; on interp and compile it fails with NATIVE-ONLY.

@topic stdlib.module.linux::io
title: -
summary: The process's standard input, output and error as one context, LinuxIO: install it with `with context linux::io::create()`; it implements io::IO.
index: stdlib
keywords: stdout; stdin; stderr; standard output; console io
related: stdlib.module.io stdlib.module.linux concept.contexts concept.context-traits

== NOTES
The functions here require the LinuxIO context and take no descriptor:
write, write_error and read, plus write_text and write_error_text, which
make LinuxIO the implementation of the portable io::IO trait. Calling one
before the context is installed is MISSING-CONTEXT. Not a direct module:
it mediates linux's raw authority through its own context. Its writes
are raw syscalls, so they run on the native backends only.

